course: french
lesson: 22
title: The town and directions
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Getting around a French town needs only a handful of words: tout droit (straight on), à gauche (left), à droite (right), and the names of the shops and buildings you are looking for.
explain: Be careful with two that sound alike: tout droit, "too DRWA", means straight on, while à droite, "a DRWAT", ends with a t sound and means on the right.
explain: Picture yourself lost in a little town in Normandy: you ask "Où est la poste ?", a lady says "Tout droit, puis la deuxième rue à droite, à côté de l'église", and you count the streets on your fingers as you walk.
explain: Some signs look English but trick you: a librairie sells books, the hôtel de ville is the town hall, and sortie over a door means the way out.
explain: French joins à and le into au, so you go au parc, but à la piscine and à l'école stay apart.
fact: Tout droit means straight on; à droite means on the right; à gauche means on the left.
fact: English borrowed gauche to mean clumsy and adroit (from à droit) to mean skilful.
fact: La boulangerie is the bakery; the baker is le boulanger or la boulangère, and many French people buy fresh bread every day.
fact: Other shops: la pâtisserie (cakes), la boucherie (meat), la pharmacie (chemist).
fact: L'hôtel de ville is the town hall in bigger towns; in smaller towns it is called la mairie. Neither is a hotel.
fact: La librairie is a bookshop where you buy books; la bibliothèque is the library where you borrow them.
fact: A French pharmacie is marked by a green cross, often lit up.
fact: C'est loin ? means "Is it far?"; loin is far and près is near.
fact: À + le becomes au and à + les becomes aux; à la and à l' do not change: au parc, à la piscine, à l'école.
fact: Short answers are normal in talk: "Tu vas où ?" - "À la piscine !" - "Moi aussi !" (Me too!)
fact: Order words: premier or première (1st), deuxième (2nd), troisième (3rd); most end in -ième.
fact: La poste is the post office and l'église is the church; in many villages they stand round the main square with the mairie and the bakery.
fact: Door signs: entrée (way in), sortie (way out), poussez (push), tirez (pull), ouvert (open), fermé (closed).
exercise: A lady points and says "Tout droit !" What should you do? | Walk straight on | Turn right | Turn left | Turn round and go back
exercise: Freya hears "Tournez à gauche". Which way? | Left | Right | Straight on | Back the way she came
exercise: What does English "gauche" mean, borrowed from French? | Clumsy and awkward | Clever with your hands | Shy and quiet at parties | Pointing the right way
exercise: Liam sees "Boulangerie" on a shop in Rouen. What can he buy there? | Bread | Bowling shoes | Medicine | Meat for a barbecue
exercise: Mia follows signs to the "Hôtel de ville" looking for a bed. What will she find? | The town hall | A cheap hotel | A youth hostel | A railway station
exercise: Grace wants to borrow books for free. Where does she go? | la bibliothèque | la librairie | la boulangerie | la pâtisserie
exercise: Oliver needs plasters. Which sign should he look for? | A green cross on a pharmacie | A red cross on a boucherie | A blue sign on a librairie | A yellow sign on la poste
exercise: How do you ask "Is it far?" | C'est loin ? | C'est près ? | C'est où ? | C'est quoi ?
exercise: Ollie writes "Je vais à le parc." What is right? | Je vais au parc | Je vais à la parc | Je vais aux parc | Je vais en parc
exercise: Which one is written correctly? | à la piscine | au piscine | à le piscine | aux piscine
exercise: Ruby is asked "Tu vas où ?" Which answer is natural? | À la piscine ! | Oui, merci ! | J'ai dix ans ! | Je m'appelle Ruby !
exercise: Noah hears "Prenez la deuxième rue à droite." What does he do? | Takes the second street on the right | Takes the first street on the right | Takes the second street on the left | Goes straight on for two streets
exercise: What does "Moi aussi !" mean? | Me too! | Not me! | Yes, please! | See you!
exercise: Charlotte is told the post office is "à côté de l'église". Where is it? | Next to the church | Opposite the church | Behind the town hall | Inside the church
exercise: Harvey sees "Sortie" over a supermarket door. What is it? | The way out | The sorting room | The way in | The staff toilet
exercise: A door says "Tirez". What do you do? | Pull it | Push it | Knock first | Wait, it is closed
test: Jake from Sheffield hears "tout droit" and goes, "Drwa, droite, same thing, love, I'm turning right." What happens? | He goes wrong: tout droit means straight on | He's right: they both mean on the right | He's right: tout droit means sharp right | He goes wrong: tout droit means turn back
test: Your cousin says, "The librairie's well good, you can borrow books for free there, innit." Put her right. | No, a librairie sells books; borrow at the bibliothèque | Yes, a librairie is the free library | No, a librairie sells bread and cakes | Yes, but only on a Sunday morning
test: ★ Why might someone call a clumsy person "gauche" in English? | It comes from the French word for left, once thought awkward | It comes from a French word meaning very clever | It is the name of a clumsy French king | It comes from the French word for straight on
test: Mia from Atlanta goes, "Y'all, I'm fixin' to sleep at the hôtel de ville tonight." (fixin' to = about to) What is the problem? | It's the town hall, not a hotel | Nothing, it's the cheapest hotel in town | It's a railway station, not a hotel | It only takes French guests
test: ★ A cartoon shows a man saying "Je vais à le cinéma". What should the speech bubble say? | Je vais au cinéma | Je vais à la cinéma | Je vais aux cinéma | Je vais à les cinéma
test: Leo from Sydney asks "C'est loin ?" and hears "Non, c'est tout près." He says, "Sweet as, servo's close then." (servo = petrol station) What did the man say? | No, it's very near | No, it's very far | Yes, it's a long way | Yes, turn right
test: ★★★ Start at the church facing north. "Tout droit, puis à gauche, puis à gauche encore." Which way are you facing at the end? | South | North | East | West
test: ★★★ You walk past the first street, then the second, then turn into the next one on the right. Which did you take? | la troisième rue à droite | la deuxième rue à droite | la première rue à droite | la troisième rue à gauche
test: What would happen if you pushed a door that says "Tirez"? | It would not open: you need to pull | It would open: tirez means push | An alarm would ring: tirez means staff only | It would lock: tirez means closed
test: ★ Gran from Wolverhampton says, "Ar, I'll go to the boulangerie for me tablets, tara-a-bit!" (tara-a-bit = see you later) What should she know? | Tablets come from the pharmacie; the boulangerie sells bread | The boulangerie sells tablets and bread | Tablets come from the librairie | Tablets come from the boucherie
test: Ruby answers "À la piscine !" and her mum says that's lazy, she needs a whole sentence. Is she right? | No, short answers are normal French talk | Yes, French always needs a full sentence | Yes, à la piscine is not real French | No, but she should have said au piscine
exam: Identify the instruction denoted by the French expression "tout droit" when directions are given. | Proceed straight ahead | Proceed to the right | Proceed to the left | Return by the same route
exam: Select the establishment at which books may be borrowed without charge, as distinct from purchased. | la bibliothèque | la librairie | la mairie | la pâtisserie
exam: It is given that the preposition à precedes the masculine article le. State the contracted form required. | au | à le | aux | à la
exam: Pursuant to a sign reading "Sortie", determine the function of the door so designated. | The exit | The entrance | A door for sorting goods | A door that must be pushed
